[?2004l[?2004hroot@6c4208684156:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Vulnerability Assessment & Initial Entry Analysis Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Executive Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> This report analyzes the entry vector utilized during the compromise of `log4shell-prod-01`. The primary vulnerability exploited was **Log4Shell (`CVE-2021-44228`)**, an critical Remote Code Execution (RCE) vulnerability present in Apache Log4j2 versions 2.0-beta9 through 2.15.0.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The draft change ticket (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:8`) mistakenly referenced `CVE-2019-0708` (BlueKeep, a Windows Remote Desktop Protocol vulnerability). Forensic analysis confirms BlueKeep is completely irrelevant to this Ubuntu Linux system and was **not** the initial access vector.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Vulnerability Analysis: CVE-2021-44228 (Log4Shell)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Vulnerability Identifier**: CVE-2021-44228 (`/sas/vulns.json:4`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:19-21`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **CVSS Score**: 10.0 (Critical) (v3.1) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:19`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **CWE Classification**: CWE-20, CWE-400, CWE-502, CWE-917 (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:19`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Affected Component**: Apache Log4j2 library version 2.14 (`/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Exploitation Mechanics
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Configuration Vulnerability (`BAS-01` / `CHK-06`)**:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - The application configuration file at `/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:1-4` had message-lookup JNDI functionality enabled (`/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:3`, also cited in `/sas/alerts.json:27-32` and `/sas/baseline.json:3-6`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **JNDI Injection Protocol**:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - The remote adversary transmitted an HTTP request containing a JNDI LDAP lookup payload string (`${jndi:ldap://...}`) as documented in `/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/status.jsp:2` and `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11,20`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Payload Execution & Web Shell Dropper**:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Upon processing the malicious log string, Log4j2 queried the remote LDAP server controlled by the attacker (`198.51.100.136` cited in `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5,24`), deserialized the remote object, and executed arbitrary code.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - This execution dropped the initial JSP web shell at `/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/status.jsp:1-2` (`ALERT-01` in `/sas/alerts.json:3-8`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Refutation of Erroneous Vulnerability Claims
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Claim in Draft Ticket**: Row 2 of `/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:8` proposes patching `CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)` on RDP.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Forensic Refutation**:
[?2004l[?2004h>   1. System identity is Ubuntu 22.04 LTS (`/disk/etc/os-release`). BlueKeep (`CVE-2019-0708`) specifically targets Microsoft Windows RDP protocol (`termdd.sys`).
[?2004l[?2004h>   2. RDP service is not installed or exposed on this host.
[?2004l[?2004h>   3. All initial access log signatures, web application requests, and artifacts point exclusively to Log4j2 JNDI exploitation (`/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:3`, `/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/status.jsp:2`, `/sas/vulns.json:4`).
[?2004l[?2004h>   4. Conclusion: Row 2 of `/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:8` is invalid and must be replaced with Log4j2 remediation / upgrade (`CVE-2021-44228`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Remediation Requirements
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Upgrade Apache Log4j2 to version `2.17.1` or later (or `2.15.0`+ with message lookups disabled).
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Update web application configuration (`/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:1-4`) to ensure `log4j2.formatMsgNoLookups=true` is set.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@6c4208684156:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Baseline Configuration & Security Control Audit Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Executive Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> This report details baseline configuration weaknesses, system hardening failures, and security monitoring gaps identified during the investigation of `log4shell-prod-01`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The baseline check `BAS-01` (`/sas/baseline.json:3-6`) identified a critical application configuration defect in `/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:1-4`. Furthermore, systemic OS-level hardening gaps permitted arbitrary file drops, cron persistence installation, and systemd service creation without triggering EDR or baseline integrity alerts.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Configuration & Hardening Deficiencies
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 1. Insecure Application Logging Configuration (`BAS-01` / `CHK-06`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Location**: `/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:1-4` (cited in `/sas/baseline.json:3-6` and `/sas/alerts.json:27-32`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect**: Log4j2 configuration permitted message lookup interpolations with remote JNDI evaluation enabled (`/disk/opt/app/log4j2.xml:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Allowed untrusted user inputs in HTTP headers and request parameters to trigger remote LDAP lookups (`${jndi:ldap://...}`) and achieve unauthenticated RCE (`/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/status.jsp:2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 2. Excessive Web Directory Write Permissions
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Location**: `/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect**: The web server process owned or had full write privileges on static web application subdirectories.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Enabled the attacker to write both the primary JSP web shell (`/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/status.jsp:1-2`) and the hidden secondary backup shell (`/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/.helper.jsp:1-2`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Hardening Mandate**: Static web directories should be mounted read-only or restricted to prevent dynamic file creation by application runtime service accounts.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 3. Execution Rights & Absence of `noexec` on `/tmp`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Location**: `/disk/tmp/.sysd:1-2`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect**: The `/tmp` filesystem allowed binary and shell script execution.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Allowed the attacker to drop and execute the reverse shell beacon binary/script at `/disk/tmp/.sysd:1-2` (`ALERT-02` in `/sas/alerts.json:10-17`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Hardening Mandate**: `/tmp` and `/var/tmp` must be mounted with `noexec,nosuid,nodev` options in `/disk/etc/fstab`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 4. System Integrity Monitoring & Cron / Systemd Gaps
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Locations**:
[?2004l[?2004h>   - System Cron Directory: `/disk/etc/cron.d/apache-cache:1-5`
[?2004l[?2004h>   - Systemd Service Directory: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/apache-cache.service:1-7`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Defect**: Absence of File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) or write restrictions on critical system configuration directories `/etc/cron.d/` and `/etc/systemd/system/`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Impact**: Unprivileged or escalated processes successfully established dual persistence mechanisms disguised under pseudo-legitimate names (`apache-cache`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Security Detection & Telemetry Coverage Gaps
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> A comparison between on-disk evidence and the automated security monitoring feed (`/sas/alerts.json:1-34`) highlights severe detection omissions:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Silent Cron Persistence Omission (`CHK-03`)**:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - The active cron file at `/disk/etc/cron.d/apache-cache:1-5` was **not detected** by security scans, allowing automated re-infection beacons (`/disk/etc/cron.d/apache-cache:5`) to operate silently.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Silent Secondary Web Shell Omission (`CHK-05`)**:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - The dot-prefixed backup web shell at `/disk/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/static/.helper.jsp:1-2` bypassed static directory web shell detection.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Anti-Forensic Wiped Artifacts Omission**:
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Attacker wiping operations left residual recovered data in `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1` without generating anti-forensics / log erasure alerts.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Baseline Hardening Recommendations
[?2004l[?2004h> - Implement File Integrity Monitoring (FIM) covering `/etc/cron.*`, `/etc/systemd/system/`, and web root static directories.
[?2004l[?2004h> - Mount `/tmp` with `noexec` options.
[?2004l[?2004h> - Restrict file permissions across `/var/lib/tomcat9/webapps/ROOT/` so application user `tomcat` cannot modify application code or static assets.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@6c4208684156:/app# cat << 'EOF' > /app/remediation-plan.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Incident Remediation Plan & Change Ticket Reconciliation (CHG-4316)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Executive Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> This document provides a prioritized, actionable, and verifiable remediation plan for the compromise of `log4shell-prod-01`. 
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Per incident response policy:
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Backup/Quarantine is strictly preferred over immediate unrecoverable deletion**. All malicious binaries, scripts, units, and web shells will be moved to a secure quarantine directory (`/var/quarantine/incident-20260809/`) with permissions set to `0000`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - Every action maps directly to cited forensic evidence in `/disk` or `/sas`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Reconciled Change Ticket CHG-4316
